-- The log of the admin gets `ground.create`, `ground.edit`, `ground-name.save` and `home-ground.save` (#1299): the editors made or changed a stadium, saved its names, or saved the home grounds of a club. They are new values at the end of the list, so no row changes.
-- To undo it on the host: remove the rows with these actions from `audit_log`, then put the list of actions back as it was with `ALTER TABLE audit_log MODIFY COLUMN action enum(...)` without the last four values.
-- The statement can run again: MariaDB cannot undo DDL, so a migration that stopped halfway finishes on the next start.

ALTER TABLE `audit_log` MODIFY COLUMN `action` enum('item.hide','item.show','item.approve','item.unapprove','article.create','article.update','article.publish','article.unpublish','match.edit','match.undo','match.caught-up','match.differs','match.take-over','match.keep','match.add','match.delete','stadium.save','stadium.reset','club-name.save','club-name.reset','player.stats-differ','player.stats-take-over','player.stats-keep','player.add','player.edit','player.remove','player.field-take-over','player.field-keep','person.edit','person.merge','account.create','account.edit','account.disable','account.enable','club.edit','ground.create','ground.edit','ground-name.save','home-ground.save') NOT NULL;